---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ImageToAvif from '@/components/tools/image-convert/ImageToAvif.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="image-to-avif">
  <ImageToAvif client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert images to AVIF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop JPG, PNG, WebP, GIF or BMP files onto the box above. The tool detects the source format from the file contents, so a wrong extension does not matter.</li>
      <li>Set the quality slider. AVIF numbers do not match JPEG numbers: quality 50 in AVIF looks about like quality 80 in JPEG.</li>
      <li>Set the effort slider for the last few percent of size. Higher effort means a longer search for a compact encoding at the same visual quality.</li>
      <li>Wait. AVIF is slow to encode, and a large photograph at high effort can take several seconds. The progress bar shows the current file.</li>
      <li>Download each file, or the whole batch as a ZIP.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The encoder is libavif with the AOM AV1 encoder, compiled to WebAssembly. It runs in a worker thread, so
      the page stays responsive. Nothing is uploaded.
    </p>

    <h2>Quality and effort: two different dials</h2>
    <p>
      People often confuse these. Quality sets the target: how much detail the encoder can discard. Effort sets
      how hard the encoder searches for the cheapest way to reach that target. Higher effort makes the file
      smaller and the encode slower. It does not make the image look better.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th align="left">AVIF quality</th>
            <th align="left">Roughly equivalent JPEG</th>
            <th align="left">Use for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>75–85</td><td>q95+</td><td>Near-transparent quality, archival delivery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60–70</td><td>q88–92</td><td>Hero images, photography portfolios</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50–60</td><td>q78–85</td><td>The general-purpose default</td></tr>
          <tr><td>35–45</td><td>q65–75</td><td>Thumbnails, list images, background textures</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Below 30</td><td>None</td><td>AVIF stays smooth, not blocky, but detail is clearly gone</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Effort 4 to 6 usually gives 3–6% smaller files for about double the time. Effort 6 to 9 gives another
      2–3% for several times the time again. Effort 6 is a sensible default. Use 2–3 for hundreds of files.
    </p>

    <h2>Where AVIF wins and where it does not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Photographs gain the most. 40–60% smaller than JPEG at matched quality is normal, and at low bitrates AVIF goes soft, where JPEG goes blocky.</li>
      <li>Gradients and flat colour are excellent. AV1's larger transform blocks and directional prediction avoid the banding of 8-bit JPEG.</li>
      <li>Transparency is supported, with the alpha plane coded separately.</li>
      <li>Logos, icons and screenshots often get larger. Lossless WebP or a well-optimised PNG usually gives a smaller file, faster.</li>
      <li>For very small images, AVIF's container overhead can make a 32 × 32 icon larger than the PNG.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Deploying AVIF safely</h2>
    <p>
      Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari 16+ and all current mobile browsers decode AVIF. Older browsers and much
      desktop software do not. The standard pattern is a picture element with AVIF first, WebP second and JPEG
      last, so the browser takes the first format it can read. Keep your originals, because AVIF is a delivery
      format. To go back, <a href="/avif-to-jpg">AVIF to JPG</a> and <a href="/avif-to-png">AVIF to PNG</a>
      convert locally. For mixed conversions in one pass, use the
      <a href="/image-converter">universal image converter</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
